{"id":"dust_motes","compound_name":"Graphs::Particles::dust_motes","source_path":"dust_motes/dust_motes.json","category":"Particles","author":"Autodesk","description":"The purpose of this graph is to show how to use Bifrost particles and instancing to simulate dust motes in the air","documentation_file":"dust_motes_docs_ENU.md","documentation":"## Dust Motes Graph\n\n### Description\n\nThis graph shows how to use the particle simulation system to create dust motes floating through the air.\n\nThe graph takes a large cube geometry to define the volume inside which the dust mote particles will be initiated.  Emission will only occur for one frame, filling the volume (volume `distribution` mode on the source_particles node,) and then allowing a turbulence influence to push the particles around, creating the desired dust mote behavior.\n\nIt's also important to get some variation at emission time on size, orientation and spin.  Hence, some \"vary\" nodes are added to the sourcing chain.  Note that orientation and spin have some specific compounds which help set the values for the complex math required internally.  This ensures that the dust motes will be of different sizes, have varying orientation at emission time, and then spin with different angles/speeds during the simulation, for a more natural look.\n\nThe final piece of this setup is assigning a geometry to be instantiated by the points, to represent the dust motes.  In this case a simple mesh plane is used.  \n\n### Inputs, Controls & Outputs\n\nThe input for this graph is a box inside which a volume of points will be spawned.  \n\nThe output is the instanced point cloud simulation.\n\n### Best Practices and Tips\n\n- The number of dust motes is controlled by the `rate` on the source_particles. Increasing this will affect performance.\n\n- modulate_influence nodes can be chained up to influences to target certain points to feel more of a certain influence.  For example having more turbulence on bigger particles vs small.  \n\n- It is convenient to use the `simulation_speed' property on the solver settings, to better control the overall speed of the effect.\n\n- Note that gravity is turned off in this graph.  For heavier dust particles which fall under force of gravity, you might want to turn it back on, but balance the gravity force with the other forces in the simulation.","library_dependencies":{"Amino":"2.16.0.2","bif":"2.16.0.2","fields":"2.16.0.2","geometries":"2.16.0.2","particles":"2.16.0.2","simulation":"2.16.0.2"},"graph_ports":[{"portName":"instances","portDirection":"output","portType":"Object"}],"terminal_states":[{"name":"Core::Graph::terminal::final","enabled":"false"},{"name":"Core::Graph::terminal::proxy","enabled":"false"},{"name":"Core::Graph::terminal::diagnostic","enabled":"false"}],"counts":{"nodes":15,"connections":15,"values":46,"compounds":1},"node_types":{"Simulation::Common::vary_source_property":3,"Simulation::Particles::set_orientation_value":2,"Value::Math::float3":2,"Simulation::Particles::particle_solver_settings":1,"Simulation::Particles::simulate_particles":1,"Geometry::Instances::set_instance_geometry":1,"Simulation::Influence::turbulence_influence":1,"Simulation::Particles::source_particles":1,"Simulation::Particles::source_particle_rotations":1,"Modeling::Primitive::create_mesh_cube":1,"Modeling::Primitive::create_mesh_plane":1},"edge_type_pairs":{"Simulation::Particles::set_orientation_value -> Simulation::Common::vary_source_property":2,"Simulation::Common::vary_source_property -> Simulation::Common::vary_source_property":2,"Value::Math::float3 -> Simulation::Common::vary_source_property":2,"Simulation::Particles::particle_solver_settings -> Simulation::Particles::simulate_particles":1,"Simulation::Influence::turbulence_influence -> Simulation::Particles::simulate_particles":1,"Simulation::Particles::simulate_particles -> Geometry::Instances::set_instance_geometry":1,"Simulation::Particles::source_particles -> Simulation::Particles::si